const LORE = [
  {"title":"The First Line","text":"Before anything, there was only the white. Then the First Scribe drew a single line, and the line became the horizon. Above it the Scribe left the sky empty. Below it the Scribe drew mountains, rivers and the long coasts, and wherever the ink touched, the land remembered it."},
  {"title":"The Eleven Hands","text":"The Scribe did not draw every people with the same hand. Frost and pine came from one, grass and horses from another, bamboo and mist, monsoon and river, savanna and drum, sand and stars, birch and snowfields, cherry and mountain, island and tide, olive and sea, terrace and condor. That is why no two peoples see the world in the same way, and why each is right."},
  {"title":"The Empire of the Quill","text":"Long before the eleven crowns, one empire spoke for the whole page. Its scholars wrote laws in a script every people could read, and its roads ran from the ice to the desert. These broken walls are what remains of it. The empire called itself the Quill, because it believed it could finish what the Scribe began."},
  {"title":"The Great Blot","text":"In the reign of the last Quill Emperor, the scholars tried to write a new land into the world. The ink would not obey. It spilled across the page in a single night, black and wet, and where it dried the land was changed. The chronicles call that night the Great Blot. Most of the chronicles end there."},
  {"title":"Ink Given Fear","text":"The Blot has no shape of its own. It takes the shape of what people fear most. In the north it rose as the restless dead, in the islands as the aswang, in the forests as the leshy, in the desert as the ghoul. Each people met the monsters of its own stories. Do not mock another land’s legends. Here, legends bite."},
  {"title":"The Sealing of the Deep Places","text":"The strongest creatures of the Blot could not be killed, so the last mages of the Quill sealed them underground: in barrows, caves, tombs and sunken temples, one in every land. The seals were written to last a thousand years. Some say it has been longer than that."},
  {"title":"The Fall of Scriptoria","text":"The Quill capital, Scriptoria, stood at the centre of the world, where all the lands meet. When the Blot came, the city was drowned in ink. Nothing of it can be seen now, but travellers who stand at the heart of the world say the ground is warmer there, and the grass grows in straight lines, like handwriting."},
  {"title":"The Eleven Crowns","text":"After the fall, the survivors gathered under eleven banners, one for each of the Scribe’s hands. They swore never to let a single empire rule the page again. They have kept that oath, mostly by fighting one another. Alliances are signed and torn, and borders are drawn in dotted lines because no one trusts them to stay."},
  {"title":"The Hunters’ Oath","text":"Carved above a doorway: “We hunt what crawls out of the Blot, in every land and under every banner. We take no side in the quarrels of crowns. A monster slain in an enemy’s field is still a monster slain.” This was the first hall of the Hunters’ Lodge."},
  {"title":"Ink in the Blood","text":"When a great creature of the Blot is destroyed, its ink does not vanish. Some of it passes into the one who struck it down. That is where the old powers come from: frost, fire, the roar, the stomp of a titan. The Quill forbade such power. The Quill is gone."},
  {"title":"A Mapmaker’s Letter","text":"“I have walked from the frozen north to the island coasts, and still my map is not finished. Every time I return to a place, a path has moved or a ruin has appeared where there was none. I begin to think the world is still being drawn, and that we are walking on wet ink.”"},
  {"title":"The Edge of the Page","text":"Sail far enough in any direction and the sea grows pale, then white. Sailors who came back said the water simply stops being drawn. The Quill scholars believed the edge of the world is not an end but a margin, waiting for someone to write in it."},
  {"title":"The Dotted Borders","text":"An old boundary stone. One side reads “Here ends the land of one crown.” The other side reads “Here begins the land of another.” Someone has scratched beneath both: “The grass does not know the difference.”"},
  {"title":"The Last Page","text":"The final stone is cracked through the middle. What can still be read says that the First Scribe left one page unwritten, hidden somewhere in the world. Whoever finds it, and holds a pen steady enough, will write how this story ends."}
];

